<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/fn/ECA18-Footnote1.1> a ipbes:Footnote ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:ECA18 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/ECA18-1.1.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature’s contributions to people encompass the positive contributions, or benefits, and occasionally negative contributions, losses or detriments, that people obtain from nature. The term resonates with the original use of the term ecosystem services in the Millenium Ecosystem Assessment (MEA, 2005), and goes further by explicitly embracing concepts associated with other worldviews on human–nature relations and knowledge systems." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Footnote1.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/fn/ECA18-Footnote1.2> a ipbes:Footnote ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:ECA18 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/ECA18-1.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The fifth national reports provide, among other aspects, an update on the national status and trends of, and threats to, biodiversity, using national biodiversity indicators and also an assessment of the progress towards the Aichi Biodiverity Targets and the implementation of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011-2020." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Footnote1.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/fn/ECA18-Footnote1.3> a ipbes:Footnote ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:ECA18 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/ECA18-1.5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In the Regional Assessment for Europe and Central Asia, the term “biodiversity” is used in different senses, from its scientific sense of biological diversity to its more encompassing sense of the natural environment in general and the concept of intrinsic value (see also Mace et al., 2012)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Footnote1.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/fn/ECA18-Footnote1.4> a ipbes:Footnote ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:ECA18 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/ECA18-1.5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The Regional Assessment for Europe and Central Asia uses both the terms “nature’s contributions to people” and “ecosystem services”. The latter is used when refering to literature dealing with specific ecosystem services, while “nature’s contributions to people” is applied to convey statements refering to the broader category of anthropocentric values (which include ecosystem services)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Footnote1.4" .

